Differences between v. 0.931 and v. 0.930
=========================================
-- 20000517 ET:


1. Madeline now uses the GNU readline library
   to provide interactive editing of text
   entered on the command line, and a command
   history buffer.  The GNU readline library
   is essentially the same one used in the 
   GNU bash shell, so other features such as
   TAB-key filename completion are also
   now conveniently provided within Madeline.
   This means, for example, that you can now
   type the first few letters of a filename
   and then press TAB:
   
     M>edit '/etc/ho[TAB]

   ... and the command line will nicely
   completed for you:
   
     M>edit '/etc/hosts'
   
   Note: Use GNU readline v. 4.1 or earlier.  I don't
   know what the GNU folk are doing with the more recent
   versions, but they don't appear to work well when trying
   to compile Madeline.  

2. Madeline now distinguishes AmericanEnglish and
   BritishEnglish in the SET LANGUAGE ... command.
   This setting affects the interpretation and 
   display of dates:

   M>set language to AmericanEnglish
   M>show {01/12/2000}
     {Wednesday, January 12, 2000}
   M>set language to BritishEnglish
   M>show {01/12/2000}
     {Friday, December 1, 2000}
   M>


3. Madeline now handles dates in ASCII flat files
   better and more intelligently.  Dates in an 
   ASCII flat file can now be read in any of these
   formats:
   
      YYYYMMDD
      MMdDDdYYYY
      DDdMMdYYYY

   ... where "YYYY" indicates digits for the year,
   "MM" digits for the month, "DD" digits for the
   day of the month, and "d" indicates any one of
   the following five delimiters:
   
     "/", "-", ".", " ", or ","
     
   (i.e., "20000112", "01/12/2000", and "01-12-2000"
   are all acceptable forms)

   The SET LANGUAGE command determines how Madeline
   interprets a delimited date string, i.e., whether
   a date is read as MMdDDdYYYY or DDdMMdYYYY.


4. Madeline now provides a new LOOKUP command which
   allows the user to quickly determine the correct
   spelling of a command or keyword by entering just
   the first few letters:
      
     M>lookup 'g'
     GENDERFIELD is an internal variable. Its current value is: "SEX".
     GENEHUNTER is a keyword.
     GENEHUNTERNPL is a keyword.
     GENEHUNTERQTL is a keyword.
     GENERIC is a keyword.
     GENOTYPE is a keyword.
     GO is a command.
     GOODBYE is a command.
     M>

   LOOKUP replaces the awkward contrivance of "SET HELP TO TEXT|HTML"
   used in previous versions. "SET HELP TO ..." is now obsolete.  You
   now use LOOKUP when you need the spelling of a command or keyword
   and use HELP when you want to pull up the HTML documentation in a
   web browser.
